19:T707,## Why two diagrams?

Low-Level Design answers two questions about a system:

- **What are the pieces, and how do they relate?** → the **class diagram** (structure).
- **How do the pieces talk to get one job done?** → the **sequence diagram** (behaviour).

Master these two and you can read, and draw, almost any design in an interview.

## The class diagram

Each **class** is a box with three parts: the **name**, its **attributes** (data), and its **operations** (methods). Visibility markers: `+` public, `-` private, `#` protected.

The arrows between boxes are the whole point, they say *who depends on whom*:

- **▷ solid (inheritance)**, *is-a*. `SavingsAccount` **extends** `Account`.
- **▷ dashed (realization)**, *implements an interface*. `Account` realizes `Statement`.
- **◆ filled diamond (composition)**, *owns, lifecycle-bound*. Destroy the `Account` and its `TransactionLog` dies with it.
- **◇ hollow diamond (aggregation)**, *has-a, shared*. The part can outlive the whole.
- **→ open arrow (association)**, *holds a reference to*.
- **⇢ dashed arrow (dependency)**, *uses transiently* (a parameter, a return type).

> Rule of thumb: prefer the **weakest** relationship that does the job. Composition over inheritance, dependency over composition. Looser coupling = easier change.

## The sequence diagram

Time flows **downward**. Each **lifeline** (the dashed vertical line) is one object's existence. A **message** is a method call drawn left-to-right:

- **solid arrow**, a synchronous **call**.
- **dashed arrow**, a **return** (the reply).
- a **self-loop**, an object calling its own method.

Read it top to bottom and you're literally reading the method calls of one use-case, in order.

That's the whole vocabulary. Hit **Watch** and see both come alive.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-intro","activeLesson":"overview"}],["$","$L18","overview",{"lesson":{"id":"overview","patternId":"lld-intro","title":"How to read these diagrams","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Intro","statement":"Two pictures power every LLD lesson, the class diagram (structure) and the sequence diagram (behaviour). Learn to read both in 5 minutes.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Uber","Atlassian"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The class diagram, structure","code":["interface Statement { print() }","","class Account implements Statement {","    - balance: int","    + withdraw(amt) { ... }","}","","class SavingsAccount extends Account { }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"A class is a box in three parts: the name on top, its private data (−) in the middle, and its public operations (+) at the bottom. Here is Account.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"stmt","name":"Statement","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ print()"],"x":0,"y":0.25},{"id":"acct","name":"Account","fields":["- balance: int","- owner: String"],"methods":["+ withdraw(amt)","+ deposit(amt)"],"x":1.15,"y":0.2},{"id":"log","name":"TransactionLog","methods":["+ record(txn)"],"x":2.35,"y":0.25},{"id":"sav","name":"SavingsAccount","methods":["+ addInterest()"],"x":1.15,"y":1.85},{"id":"notif","name":"Notifier","methods":["+ send(msg)"],"x":2.35,"y":1.85}],"relations":[{"id":"inh","from":"sav","to":"acct","kind":"inherit","label":"is-a"},{"id":"impl","from":"acct","to":"stmt","kind":"implement"},{"id":"comp","from":"acct","to":"log","kind":"compose","label":"owns","toMult":"1"},{"id":"dep","from":"acct","to":"notif","kind":"depend","label":"uses"}],"shownClassIds":["acct"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["acct"]},"vars":[["+ / − / #","public / private / protected"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":8,"narration":"A solid arrow with a hollow triangle means INHERITANCE, “is-a”. SavingsAccount extends Account, so it gets withdraw/deposit for free and adds addInterest().","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["acct","sav"],"shownRelIds":["inh"],"activeIds":["sav"]},"vars":[["▷ solid","inherits (is-a)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"A DASHED triangle means realization, a class implements an interface. Account promises the Statement contract (print()), without inheriting any code.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["acct","sav","stmt"],"shownRelIds":["inh","impl"],"activeIds":["stmt"]},"vars":[["▷ dashed","implements interface"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"A FILLED diamond is COMPOSITION, strong ownership. The Account owns its TransactionLog: no Account, no log. The diamond sits on the owner’s end.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["acct","sav","stmt","log"],"shownRelIds":["inh","impl","comp"],"activeIds":["log"]},"vars":[["◆ diamond","owns (lifecycle-bound)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"A dashed open arrow is a DEPENDENCY, the weakest link. Account merely uses Notifier transiently (a parameter), so they stay loosely coupled.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["acct","sav","stmt","log","notif"],"shownRelIds":["inh","impl","comp","dep"],"activeIds":["notif"]},"vars":[["⇢ dashed","uses (depends-on)"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"That’s the whole structure on one canvas, every class and exactly how each one is bound to the others. This static picture is what an interviewer wants first.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["acct","sav","stmt","log","notif"],"shownRelIds":["inh","impl","comp","dep"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["structure","who knows whom"]]}]},{"label":"The sequence diagram, behaviour","code":["user.withdraw(100):","    account.withdraw(100)","    account.checkBalance()","    log.record(txn)","    return receipt"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":1,"narration":"Now behaviour. Each dashed vertical line is a LIFELINE, one object existing through time. Time flows downward. Here: a User, an Account, and its TransactionLog.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"user","label":"User","kind":"actor"},{"id":"acct","label":"Account"},{"id":"log","label":"TransactionLog"}],"messages":[{"id":"s1","from":"user","to":"acct","label":"withdraw(100)","kind":"call"},{"id":"s2","from":"acct","to":"acct","label":"checkBalance()","kind":"self"},{"id":"s3","from":"acct","to":"log","label":"record(txn)","kind":"call"},{"id":"s4","from":"log","to":"acct","label":"ok","kind":"return"},{"id":"s5","from":"acct","to":"user","label":"receipt","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":0,"activeIndex":-1},"vars":[["lifeline","one object over time"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"A solid arrow is a synchronous CALL, one object invoking another’s method. The User calls account.withdraw(100).","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["→ solid","method call"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"An arrow that loops back to its own lifeline is a SELF-CALL, the object calling its own private method. Account checks its own balance.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["↺ self","own method"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"Account delegates to its TransactionLog, record(txn). Following these arrows top-to-bottom literally traces the method calls of the use-case.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["delegation","pass work to a collaborator"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"A DASHED arrow is a RETURN, control (and a result) flowing back. The log replies “ok”.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["↩ dashed","return / reply"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":5,"narration":"Finally Account returns the receipt to the User. You just read an entire interaction as a story. Structure + behaviour, that’s the whole toolkit for this track.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":5,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["done","one use-case, fully traced"]]}]}]}}]]}]
